<div class="cell-output cell-output-stdout">
<pre><code>BenchmarkResult
  ├ model       : mnist_manual
  ├ backend     : ADTypes.AutoEnzyme(mode=EnzymeCore.ReverseMode{false, false, false, EnzymeCore.FFIABI, false, false}())
  ├ n_params    : 7065
  ├ parameters  : 31.160 ms (33 allocs: 13.486 MiB)
  ├ logjoint    : 33.564 ms (24 allocs: 15.626 MiB)
  ├ lp          : 33.299 ms (24 allocs: 15.626 MiB)
  ├ lp_and_grad : 78.949 ms (68 allocs: 32.678 MiB)
  └ grad / lp   : 2.371</code></pre>
</div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display" data-execution_count="1">
<pre><code>BenchmarkResult
  ├ model       : mnist_manual
  ├ backend     : ADTypes.AutoEnzyme(mode=EnzymeCore.ReverseMode{false, false, false, EnzymeCore.FFIABI, false, false}())
  ├ n_params    : 7065
  ├ parameters  : 31.160 ms (33 allocs: 13.486 MiB)
  ├ logjoint    : 33.564 ms (24 allocs: 15.626 MiB)
  ├ lp          : 33.299 ms (24 allocs: 15.626 MiB)
  ├ lp_and_grad : 78.949 ms (68 allocs: 32.678 MiB)
  └ grad / lp   : 2.371</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We can see here that the median time has decreased by a small percentage, which is a good start. More subtly, the number of allocations has also decreased substantially. This in turn means that the garbage collector has less work to do, and reduces the spikiness in the time taken to evaluate the log-density and its gradient. While this is not obvious from the summarised benchmark that <code>@bmodel</code> gives us, you can see this if you were to directly profile the <code>WrenCore.lp_and_grad</code> function.</p>
<p>This is all very good. <em>However</em>, in the process of doing this optimisation, we have also made the model much less readable. It’s hard to see at a glance that this is indeed a multinomial regression model. This rather defeats the purpose of using a PPL in the first place: if you wanted to just write a bare mathematical expression, you could have done that in a single function without needing the abstraction of a PPL!</p>
</section>
<section id="optimisation-part-2-using-batch" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="optimisation-part-2-using-batch">Optimisation, part 2: using <code>@batch</code></h2>
<p>This is where one of Wren’s features comes in: the <code>@batch</code> macro. We can simply annotate our original <code>for</code> loop with the macro, and Wren will attempt to vectorise the likelihood evaluation:</p>
<div id="14" class="cell" data-execution_count="1">
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb14" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode julia code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode julia"><span id="cb14-1"><span class="pp" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@wren</span> <span class="kw" style="color: #003B4F;
background-color: null;
font-weight: bold;
font-style: inherit;">function</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">mnist_batch</span>(X, y, C)</span>
<span id="cb14-2">    N, D <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">size</span>(X)</span>
<span id="cb14-3">    <span class="pp" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@iid</span> W <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">~</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">Normal</span>() size<span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span>(C<span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">-</span><span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>, D)  <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># Weights</span></span>
<span id="cb14-4">    <span class="pp" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@iid</span> b <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">~</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">Normal</span>() size<span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span>(C<span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">-</span><span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>)     <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># Biases</span></span>
<span id="cb14-5">    ref_logits <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">zeros</span>(<span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">1</span>, N)                  <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">#     1 x N</span></span>
<span id="cb14-6">    nonref_logits <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> W <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">*</span> X<span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">'</span> <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">.+</span> b               <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># (C-1) x N</span></span>
<span id="cb14-7">    logits <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">vcat</span>(ref_logits, nonref_logits)  <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">#     C x N</span></span>
<span id="cb14-8">    probabilities <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
