<span id="cb14-13">model_batch <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">mnist_batch</span>(X, y, <span class="fl" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">10</span>) <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">|</span> (; y <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> y)</span>
<span id="cb14-14"><span class="pp" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@bmodel</span> model_batch DEFAULT_AD</span></code></pre></div></div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-stdout">
<pre><code>BenchmarkResult
  ├ model       : mnist_batch
  ├ backend     : ADTypes.AutoEnzyme(mode=EnzymeCore.ReverseMode{false, false, false, EnzymeCore.FFIABI, false, false}())
  ├ n_params    : 7065
  ├ parameters  : 33.645 ms (46 allocs: 19.471 MiB)
  ├ logjoint    : 33.747 ms (25 allocs: 19.282 MiB)
  ├ lp          : 33.624 ms (25 allocs: 19.282 MiB)
  ├ lp_and_grad : 79.115 ms (68 allocs: 39.522 MiB)
  └ grad / lp   : 2.353</code></pre>
</div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display" data-execution_count="1">
<pre><code>BenchmarkResult
  ├ model       : mnist_batch
  ├ backend     : ADTypes.AutoEnzyme(mode=EnzymeCore.ReverseMode{false, false, false, EnzymeCore.FFIABI, false, false}())
  ├ n_params    : 7065
  ├ parameters  : 33.645 ms (46 allocs: 19.471 MiB)
  ├ logjoint    : 33.747 ms (25 allocs: 19.282 MiB)
  ├ lp          : 33.624 ms (25 allocs: 19.282 MiB)
  ├ lp_and_grad : 79.115 ms (68 allocs: 39.522 MiB)
  └ grad / lp   : 2.353</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The only difference between this model and the original <code>mnist1</code> model is the addition of the <code>@batch</code> macro. We see that this allows us to gain the same performance benefits as when we handwrote our likelihood, with no loss in readability.</p>
<p>Hand-written likelihoods, like the one we wrote above, can have a significant impact if it allows us to reduce the number of redundant computations. For example, if we have <code>y[i] ~ Normal(mu[i], sigma)</code> for a large dataset <code>y</code>, the <code>log(sigma)</code> term does not need to be computed <code>N</code> times, and can be factored out of the likelihood. Just like in our present example, <code>@batch</code> <em>also</em> does such optimisations automatically for you, without you having to write out the likelihood by hand!</p>
